==== stdout ====
raphael help connecting - Start a local server and connect a client.

Run a server: put RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY=<key> in an owner-only .env file, made with (umask 077 && touch .env && chmod 600 .env && echo "RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)" > .env), then run raphael server serve in that directory. With --config, the .env beside the configuration file is read instead. An environment value wins over .env, and an empty one is refused rather than ignored. The server listens on http://127.0.0.1:3000 unless --host, --port or the configuration file say otherwise, and prints the address and the login command when it starts. It never reads your saved login.

Connect: in another terminal run raphael login http://127.0.0.1:3000 and enter the same key. Login verifies the address and key before saving them with owner-only permissions to $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/raphael/config.json, or ~/.config/raphael/config.json. Without a terminal, pipe the key: printf '%s\n' "$KEY" | raphael login <address> --api-key-stdin. Logging in again replaces the saved login; raphael logout removes it.

Check: raphael status reports the server, where its address and key came from, and the protocol and release of each side. Run it first in a script; exit 0 means remote commands can work.

Without a saved login, set RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT and RAPHAEL_API_KEY together. Both or neither: a partial pair is refused, an empty variable counts as unset, and a complete pair overrides the saved login. RAPHAEL_API_KEY is the server's RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY; clients do not read .env, and the server does not read RAPHAEL_API_KEY. A key must be visible ASCII characters with no spaces.

RAPHAEL_TIMEOUT sets the request timeout in whole seconds, from 1 to 120; the default is 15 seconds. Exit 5 means the connection needs attention: the error names the address and where it came from, and the hint says what to change. Exit 6 means the server could not be reached and nothing was applied. Read help exit-codes before retrying a failed write.

Examples:
  raphael server serve
  raphael login http://127.0.0.1:3000
  raphael status --json

Next: raphael help reference
